Applying the signature-as-a-sentence approach: "parkVehicle takes a vehicle, returns a ticket, and raises an exception if the lot has no space for that vehicle's size."

```java
public Ticket parkVehicle(Vehicle vehicle) throws LotFullException {
    ParkingSpot spot = findAvailableSpot(vehicle.getSize());
    if (spot == null) {
        throw new LotFullException(vehicle.getSize());
    }
    spot.assign(vehicle);
    return new Ticket(vehicle, spot, Instant.now());
}
```

A checked exception here is a deliberate choice: a full lot is an expected, recoverable condition the caller must handle — such as showing a "lot full" message — not a bug, which is exactly the distinction the next slide's comparison covers.
